      { id: "doc_200_01", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 200, prompt: "What is the name for the deepest zone of the ocean, found in trenches below 6,000 meters?", answer: "The hadal zone / hadopelagic zone", acceptableAnswers: ["Hadal zone", "Hadopelagic zone", "Hadal"], explanation: "Named after Hades, the hadal zone includes ocean trenches where pressures exceed 1,000 atmospheres." },
      { id: "doc_200_02", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 200, prompt: "What is bioluminescence, the ability many deep-sea creatures use to produce their own light?", answer: "The production of light by living organisms through chemical reactions (typically luciferin + luciferase)", acceptableAnswers: ["Chemical light production", "Living things making their own light", "Biological light production"], explanation: "Up to 90% of deep-sea organisms produce bioluminescence — it's the rule, not the exception, in the deep ocean." },
      { id: "doc_200_03", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 200, prompt: "What are hydrothermal vents, the deep-sea features that support life without sunlight?", answer: "Fissures in the ocean floor that release superheated mineral-rich water, supporting chemosynthetic ecosystems independent of photosynthesis", acceptableAnswers: ["Hot water vents on ocean floor", "Volcanic vents supporting life", "Seafloor vents with chemosynthetic life"], explanation: "Discovered in 1977, hydrothermal vents revolutionized biology by proving that life could exist entirely without sunlight." },
      { id: "doc_400_01", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 400, prompt: "What is chemosynthesis, and how does it differ from photosynthesis as a basis for an ecosystem?", answer: "Chemosynthetic bacteria use chemical energy from hydrogen sulfide or methane (instead of sunlight) to produce organic molecules, forming the base of vent food chains", acceptableAnswers: ["Chemical energy instead of light", "Using hydrogen sulfide for energy", "Chemical-based food production"], explanation: "Chemosynthesis proved that sunlight isn't necessary for complex ecosystems, revolutionizing our understanding of where life can exist — including potentially on other worlds." },
      { id: "doc_400_02", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 400, prompt: "What is a 'whale fall,' and why is it an important deep-sea ecosystem?", answer: "A whale carcass that sinks to the deep ocean floor, creating a localized ecosystem that can sustain specialized organisms for 50-100 years through progressive decomposition stages", acceptableAnswers: ["Sunken whale carcass ecosystem", "Dead whale creating ocean floor ecosystem", "Whale carcass supporting life for decades"], explanation: "Whale falls go through stages: first scavengers strip the flesh, then smaller organisms colonize the bones, and finally chemosynthetic bacteria break down the lipid-rich skeleton." },
      { id: "doc_400_03", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 400, prompt: "What deep-sea creature can withstand extreme heat, extreme cold, and crushing pressure, making it a candidate for studying extraterrestrial life?", answer: "Giant tube worms (Riftia pachyptila) at hydrothermal vents", acceptableAnswers: ["Giant tube worms", "Riftia pachyptila", "Tube worms"], explanation: "Giant tube worms have no mouth, gut, or anus — they survive entirely through symbiotic chemosynthetic bacteria living inside their bodies." },
      { id: "doc_600_01", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 600, prompt: "What is the 'iron hypothesis' regarding deep-sea hydrothermal vents and the origin of life on Earth?", answer: "Iron-sulfur minerals at alkaline hydrothermal vents could have catalyzed the first organic reactions, creating natural electrochemical gradients similar to those used by all living cells for energy production", acceptableAnswers: ["Iron-sulfur catalysis at vents started life", "Alkaline vent origin of life", "Electrochemical gradients from vents creating first life"], explanation: "The alkaline hydrothermal vent theory (by Mike Russell and Bill Martin) proposes that life began at deep-sea vents where natural proton gradients across mineral walls drove the first biochemistry." },
      { id: "doc_600_02", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 600, prompt: "What specific adaptation allows the Mariana snailfish to survive at nearly 8,200 meters depth, and what biochemical modification makes this possible?", answer: "High concentrations of trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO) stabilize its proteins against crushing pressure; its bones are barely mineralized and its skull is not fully closed, reducing rigid structures that pressure could damage", acceptableAnswers: ["TMAO protein stabilization", "TMAO and reduced bone mineralization", "Trimethylamine N-oxide for pressure resistance"], explanation: "TMAO acts as a 'chemical chaperone' that prevents proteins from being crushed into non-functional shapes by the immense pressure — the deeper the fish, the higher the TMAO concentration." },
      { id: "doc_600_03", categoryId: "sci_deep_ocean", tier: 600, prompt: "What controversy surrounds deep-sea mining of polymetallic nodules, and what specific ecosystem would be destroyed?", answer: "Nodules containing manganese, nickel, cobalt, and rare earths sit on abyssal plains and take millions of years to form; mining would destroy the organisms that live on and around them — a slow-growing ecosystem that may take centuries or millennia to recover, if ever", acceptableAnswers: ["Mining slow-forming nodules destroys irreplaceable ecosystems", "Polymetallic nodule mining vs abyssal ecosystems", "Million-year-old nodules supporting unique life"], explanation: "The ISA is considering allowing deep-sea mining despite scientists warning that abyssal ecosystems are so slow to develop that any destruction is effectively permanent on human timescales." }
